"""Source reconciliation, distinct from the independent Sol arithmetic audit."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,html
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
d=json.loads((ROOT/'data/evidence/literature-crosschecks.json').read_text())
css='''body{margin:0;background:#f6f4ee;color:#1f2733;font:15px/1.65 -apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Arial,sans-serif}main{max-width:1000px;margin:auto;padding:42px 35px 60px}h1,h2{font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#16284a}h1{font-size:38px;line-height:1.2}h2{font-size:25px;margin:35px 0 12px}.lead{font-size:19px}.eyebrow{font-size:11px;letter-spacing:1px}a{color:#16284a;text-underline-offset:3px}nav{display:flex;gap:24px;padding:15px 0 24px;border-bottom:2px solid #16284a}.table{overflow:auto}table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;background:white;font-size:13px}th,td{padding:11px 12px;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd7c9}th{background:#16284a;color:white;font-weight:500}pre{padding:16px;background:#efece3;white-space:pre-wrap}@media(max-width:650px){main{padding:24px 18px}h1{font-size:30px}nav{gap:15px;font-size:13px}}'''
sources=[
('Maryland OPC complaint, May 7, 2026','https://opc.maryland.gov/Portals/0/Files/Publications/Others/20260507%20-%20RTEP%20complaint%20-%20FINAL%20932.pdf','Printed p3 Tables1-2; pp55-61; supporting affidavit','Estimates $2.001bn of Maryland capital allocation and $1.621bn of ten-year revenue requirements across three RTEP windows. Its crediting proposal is not proof of permanent additive pricing. These are complainant estimates, not adjudicated damages or a national score.'),
('PJM answer, July 27, 2026','https://elibrary.ferc.gov/eLibrary/filelist?accession_number=20260727-5242&optimized=false','pp21-28; docket accession20260727-5242','Opposes OPC: regional upgrades provide reliability, congestion and generation-access benefits beyond the load that triggers construction. Says existing beneficiary-pays precedent and state retail allocation must be addressed. These are opposing arguments, not a Commission disposition.'),
('UCS, Data Center Power Play, January 2026','https://www.ucs.org/sites/default/files/2026-01/Data-Center-Power-Play-report-final.pdf','pp7, 9, 14-15; technical appendix pp4-9','Estimates $886-978bn of wholesale electricity costs attributable to DC growth during 2026-2050 in its mid-demand policy cases. This includes supplying electricity, not just transmission, and is neither the portion shifted to others nor BAAJA-additional receipts.'),
('WIRES / Concentric, March 2026','https://wiresgroup.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Competitive-Transmission-Report-2026-by-CEA.pdf','Printed pp7-8, 13-17; footnotes10-14','Compares ordinary NITS with upgrades-only payments plus NITS exemption. Its gross capital benchmark is $23.3bn /20GW, about $1.16m/MW. The modeled NITS-growth and customer-lifetime assumptions are material; its headline discounted cost shifts cannot be relabeled as our undiscounted policy score.'),
('Harvard Electricity Law Initiative reply, April 15, 2026','https://eelp.law.harvard.edu/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Harvard-ELI-RM26-4-Reply-to-WIRES.pdf','pp1-4 and proposed alternatives','Explains that ordinary embedded pricing can initially under-recover upgrades, while an upgrades-only exemption can later under-contribute to the shared system. A 30-year crossover assumes customers remain; it is not empirical national capital calibration.'),
('Virginia JLARC, Data Centers in Virginia, December 2024','https://jlarc.virginia.gov/pdfs/reports/Rpt598-2.pdf','Printed pp44-47; AppendixB pp100-101','Finds current rates recover allocated costs for the studied Virginia utilities, while future growth can raise other customers costs. Its future model omits much intrazonal transmission. A fully allocated cost-of-service finding is compatible with rising marginal system costs.'),
('E3, Tailored for Scale, December 2025','https://www.ethree.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/RatepayerStudy.pdf','Printed pp1-3, 26-28','Amazon-funded study of sites in four utility territories reports projected surplus revenues. Cases exclude facility-exclusive infrastructure already paid upfront and do not model system-wide supply/demand changes. It is useful counterevidence, not a nationally representative estimate.'),
('PJM Independent Market Monitor comments, July 27, 2026','https://www.monitoringanalytics.com/filings/2026/IMM_Comments_re_Complaint_Docket_No_EL26-63_20260727.pdf','pp3-5','Supports direct charging of identifiable DC-driven transmission costs. It supports a cost-allocation concern, not a particular national dollar estimate.'),
('Grid Strategies, Federal Transmission Pricing, Volume2, June2026','https://gridstrategiesllc.com/wp-content/uploads/Federal-Transmission-Pricing-Vol-2_Report_FINAL.pdf','Printed pp12-13; options discussion','Distinguishes wholesale allocation to LSEs from retail recovery from customers. Regional rules, retail tariffs and shared project benefits need to be reconciled; an LSE payment is not automatically a dollar-for-dollar residential bill reduction.'),
('BAAJA discussion draft, section2107(a), September2026','https://www.energy.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Bipartisan-American-Affordability-and-Jobs-Act.pdf','pp227-230','Requires embedded and incremental transmission costs with incremental receipts credited against applicable utility ATRR; also requires lower-cost nonfirm service. Implementation, attribution, and existing versus new service are not fully specified by the draft.')]
body='''<p class="eyebrow">DASHBOARD AMERICAN · SOURCE REVIEW · 24 OCTOBER 2026</p><h1>Evidence behind the BAAJA estimate</h1>
<p class="lead">The evidence supports a serious cost-allocation problem. It does not establish a nationally calibrated $75bn BAAJA score. A U.S. demand-path cross-check gives roughly $64-69bn under the same selected capital and financial assumptions, before offsets for existing recovery.</p>
<nav><a href="index.html">Calculator</a><a href="evidence.html">Project evidence</a><a href="https://github.com/dashboardamerican/baaja-and-pricing">Code &amp; data</a></nav>
<h2>Three different dollar measures</h2><p><b>System cost</b> is what it costs to build and operate the electricity system. <b>Customer payments</b> are what tariffs and contracts collect. <b>Additional relief</b> is the change in payments caused by the policy, credited to other customers. These quantities cannot be substituted for one another.</p>
<p>The dashboard's second measure is <b>payment above modeled incremental transmission cost</b>. It includes the ordinary contribution to the shared grid and is not proof of overcharging, utility profit, payment above fully allocated cost of service, or federal tax revenue. The brief now uses the narrower label.</p>
<h2>Source reconciliation</h2><div class="table"><table><thead><tr><th>Primary source / locator</th><th>What it supports—and its limit</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''
for title,url,locator,claim in sources:
    body+=f'<tr><td><a href="{html.escape(url,quote=True)}">{html.escape(title)}</a><br>{html.escape(locator)}</td><td>{html.escape(claim)}</td></tr>'
body+='''</tbody></table></div>
<h2>Two different kinds of credit</h2><p>OPC discusses returning upfront upgrade funding to the customer through appropriate credits, drawing on generator-interconnection protections. That can prevent permanent double charging. BAAJA section2107(a) instead expressly credits incremental receipts against the utility's annual transmission revenue requirement. That reduces shared recovery from other customers. The word “credit” alone does not make the mechanisms equivalent. PJM disputes OPC's allocation theory: a project triggered by DC growth may also provide wider regional benefits. A defensible attribution rule must evaluate that claim; it cannot equate every DC-related project dollar with a subsidy.</p>
<h2>Published U.S. demand through 2050</h2><p>UCS provides state-level IT-plus-cooling inputs on GitHub. Summing those inputs gives the following U.S. totals. Its “current policy” and “central” paths are almost identical for data-center demand; their wider electrification assumptions differ. UCS's capacity and project-realization assumptions are scenarios, not measured future demand. Crypto coverage is not presumed identical to EPRI's.</p><div class="table"><table><thead><tr><th>UCS input path</th><th>2030 TWh</th><th>2050 TWh</th><th>Additional, source-derived 2026 baseline</th><th>Additional, common 300 TWh baseline</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''
for row in d['UCS_path_sensitivities']:
    body+=f"<tr><td>{html.escape(row['source_scenario'])}</td><td>{row['source_points_TWh']['2030']:,.0f}</td><td>{row['source_points_TWh']['2050']:,.0f}</td><td>${row['source_2026_baseline_case']['A_billion_2026_USD']:.1f}bn</td><td>${row['common_300_TWh_2026_baseline_case']['A_billion_2026_USD']:.1f}bn</td></tr>"
body+='''</tbody></table></div><p>Payment calculations retain $0.65m/MW, 80% load factor, 75% modeled-grid participation, the 7.33% real annual cost factor, national pooling, and no prior-payment offset. We interpolate linearly between published years and select 1.5% annual demand growth for 2051-2056 in every UCS case. These are our model outputs, not UCS estimates of BAAJA receipts. The source-derived baseline is interpolated from 2025 and 2030; it is not observed 2026 use. The common-baseline case instead connects our 300 TWh assumption to the published 2030 point.</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/ucsusa/eer_load_shapes/blob/7b980999a8f6e63172b834494b785f452b95fffb/UCS_load_profile_scaling/scaling_inputs_MWh.csv">Pinned UCS inputs</a> · <a href="data/evidence/ucs-demand-inputs-MWh.csv">Archived input table</a> · <a href="data/evidence/literature-crosschecks.json">Calculations and assumptions</a> · <a href="data/evidence/literature-sources.json">Source manifest and hashes</a>.</p>
<h2>The regional model still needs actual project allocations</h2><p>The existing $44.2bn separate-zone calculation assumes each zone bears all modeled incremental cost of its own new load. It does not replay PJM Schedule12's project-specific load-ratio-share/DFAX allocations. The 14% pooling difference is therefore a synthetic comparison, not a measured correction to national receipts. OPC's complaint specifically concerns costs allocated outside the host zone.</p>
<p>A toy example shows why it matters: one zone has 100 old MW plus 100 new MW, another has 100 old MW, and incremental cost is $100. Allocating that cost entirely to the host zone makes the new customer pay $50 through embedded charges, leaving $50 of policy uplift. Allocating half to each zone makes it pay $25, leaving $75 of uplift. Changing where projects are assigned can change national additional receipts even with identical physical investment.</p>
<h2>What should determine a defensible score</h2><p>Build a project-cohort ledger with the attributable share of each upgrade, approved annual revenue requirements and in-service dates; follow its allocation through regional, zonal, LSE and retail rates; reconcile CIAC, refunds, security agreements and minimum bills; and measure actual billing demand, nonfirm service and exits. Net existing payments once—capital already reduced for customer funding must not receive a second deduction for the same funding.</p>
<p>Better NITS evidence principally improves the existing-grid contribution measure. With embedded pricing and fixed billing shares, additional receipts equal <code>(1 − s) × I</code> and do not depend on the inherited NITS rate. Cost attribution, prior recovery and the applicable billing pools remain the critical inputs.</p>
<p>Use the current result as a conditional planning benchmark, not a validated tax-like score. The literature review does not justify adding UCS system costs or OPC capital allocations to it, nor converting them into a confidence interval. The transmission ITC comparison remains an analyst extension of a historical ten-year federal budget score; private bill transfers are not federal offsets.</p>
<h2>Review status</h2><p>This is Codex's source reconciliation and reproducible cross-check. The earlier independent Sol audit checked arithmetic and selected data joins. A separate Claude review has not run: the requested independent review is still pending. No Claude conclusions are represented here.</p>'''
(ROOT/'literature-review.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>BAAJA · Literature reconciliation</title><style>'+css+'</style></head><body><main>'+body+'</main></body></html>')
print(ROOT/'literature-review.html')
